Z||000005||Hank is Here||12-15-2000||11:54 PM||||My fathers 88GT had a simliar problem but a little different.  He would be driving and the wipers would go, this became more and more a common occurance over a year or so till they swiped every 30 seconds or so. Turned out to be a bad circuit board, took it to an electronics fellow and he "fixed" the problem.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||12-16-2000||12:25 AM||||I have a 86 SE V6 and it does the same thing. I have looked at all the forum members sugestions on possible fix and there is so many different choices that i just gave up and pulled the fuse. I dont drive it in the rain anyway. PS I really think it is in the circuit board in the wiper motor.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bob Englert||12-16-2000||01:00 PM||||RossT:<P>Rodney does NOT sell a repalcement wiper switch to fix the problem with the wiper when you use your turn signal.  Rodney sells the wiper stalk with the cruise switches, but this HAS NOTHING to do with the wiper behavior you mentioned.  <P>The wiper switch is actually inside the steering column and works together with the turn signal switch (also inside the column).  The turn signal stalk acts like a big stick that allows you to operate the wiper and turn signals.  Except for the washer control, you could actually use a stick to operate the wiper and turn signal.<P>The fix for random wiper actions is usually 1 or 2 items: 1) replace the wiper control board (easy fix), or 2) replace the wiper switch in the column (not so easy).  My daughters 87 coupe had a wiper that would only go on at full speed.  After replacing the switch, the wipers have worked fine since.<P>Rodney's stalk works great when replacing a broken stalk or if you have a bad wire in the cruise switch.  Just used his part on my 87 GT.  Excellent deal.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000008||Zorba||12-16-2000||01:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bob Englert:<BR><B>RossT: The fix for random wiper actions is usually 1 or 2 items: 1) replace the wiper control board (easy fix  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Good suggestion, but can you give details on how to do this easy fix? Where is the wiper control board? Removing the fuse is a good idea if it isn't raining anyway. But I would rather have it fixed than compromise.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||12-16-2000||03:50 PM||||I've had two fieros that had ramdom wipeage when the turn signal was used. In both cases I replace the delay module (circuit board) and the problem went away. The board is mounted in the wiper motor under a plastic cover held on by one screw. It's the easiest thing to try first.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Zorba||12-17-2000||09:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>I've had two fieros that had ramdom wipeage when the turn signal was used. In both cases I replace the delay module (circuit board) and the problem went away. The board is mounted in the wiper motor under a plastic cover held on by one screw. It's the easiest thing to try first.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dumb question then...do you have to remove the wiper arms to get to it...it's cold, too lazy to go out and look.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroJoe||12-17-2000||09:59 PM||||There is also a delay module inside the car, you just have to remove the lower trim piece under the steering wheel, get inside and lay on your back looking up, and mounted way up there to the left of the steering column should be a little silver box about 2"x2".  Its got some heat sinks on it, I believe this also could be a part to replace.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
